      {
        "narrative": "<p>This assessment consisted of standard gillnet sets which were set above and below the thermocline, and small mesh smelt nets which were set below the thermocline.  No fish were caught in the smelt nets.Water quality testing showed the thermocline was from 15' to 30', with lots of oxygen present to near the bottom in each of the major basins.  Crooked Lake has the same proportion of shallow area and similar chemical characteristics as the 34 lakes in this ecological lake class.  However, Crooked Lake is larger, deeper, and less clear than most of these lakes.The total net catch (all species combined) showed above average fish populations dominated by walleye, cisco, and northern pike.  Walleye numbers were average for this lake class.  The average size of walleye, 1.3 lb, was average for the lake class.  Walleye up to 26\" were present.  Most walleye were age 3 or older; a much higher proportion of older walleye was present in Crooked Lake than in most lakes.  Reproduction was poor in 1993 and 1994.  Growth of young walleye was very slow (four year old walleye were 11\" long).  Older walleye grew faster, probably because they reached a size where they could prey on cisco.  Cisco numbers were also average.  Cisco sizes ranged from 5\" to 17\".  Northern pike numbers were much above average.  The average size of northerns was 3.2 lb, which is average.  Northern pike growth was average.Yellow perch, white sucker, rock bass, and burbot were all present in average numbers for this lake class.  Lake whitefish numbers were lower than average.  Low numbers of black crappie and smallmouth bass were captured in the 1983 assessment, but none were caught in 1995.  Smallmouth bass are known to be more abundant than is evident from the gillnet catch.  Sauger were present in fair numbers, but were not as abundant as walleye.  The largest sauger was 16\", while the largest walleye was 26\".  Natural reproduction of sauger and perch was poor from 1992 through 1994.</p>",
